WEBVTT

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.493
Data connectors bring data from other systems into your site, so reports and

00:00:05.493 --> 00:00:08.780
data pipelines can use it next to the site's own data.

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:10.862
This video is about connections:

00:00:10.862 --> 00:00:14.424
the kinds of system you can reach, how to add one and check

00:00:14.424 --> 00:00:18.040
it, how secrets are kept, and what a failed check tells you.

00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:26.207
Reports often need data that lives somewhere else:

00:00:26.207 --> 00:00:31.430
a sales system's web API, a spreadsheet someone exports every week, a search

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:33.150
index, or a database.

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.606
A connection is how the site reaches one of those systems.

00:00:37.606 --> 00:00:41.892
You set it up once, with its address and credentials, and every data

00:00:41.892 --> 00:00:44.150
set that reads from it shares it.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:48.005
There are four kinds of connection:

00:00:48.005 --> 00:00:53.610
SQL Server, HTTP API, Elasticsearch, and files in the media library.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.311
A data set then picks one table, API path, index or file from the connection.

00:01:00.311 --> 00:01:05.330
Data sets have their own video; this one stays with connections.

00:01:09.499 --> 00:01:13.561
Connections live under Tools, Data Connectors, Connections.

00:01:13.561 --> 00:01:16.749
This site has none yet, and the list says so.

00:01:17.203 --> 00:01:18.794
Click Add Connection.

00:01:18.794 --> 00:01:23.271
The site asks which kind of system the connection should reach, with a

00:01:23.271 --> 00:01:24.743
card for each kind.

00:01:24.743 --> 00:01:27.983
When the list grows, type in Filter to narrow it.

00:01:32.178 --> 00:01:36.217
The simplest kind reads files from the media library.

00:01:36.217 --> 00:01:41.421
In Media, Library, a folder named demo data holds a customers file and

00:01:41.421 --> 00:01:44.296
a products file, both in CSV format.

00:01:44.296 --> 00:01:49.089
Excel files upload as they are; for CSV, JSON and JSON Lines, an

00:01:49.089 --> 00:01:54.498
administrator first allows those file types in the site's media settings.

00:01:54.949 --> 00:01:59.221
Back in the connections, add a Media library files connection.

00:01:59.221 --> 00:02:03.745
Give it a name, Demo files, and the folder it may read, demo data.

00:02:03.745 --> 00:02:08.269
Its data sets can read any file in that folder and its subfolders.

00:02:08.719 --> 00:02:10.468
Click Save and test.

00:02:10.468 --> 00:02:14.367
The site saves the connection and checks it at once.

00:02:14.367 --> 00:02:18.199
The green message says it found two readable files.

00:02:22.399 --> 00:02:25.175
Next, a connection to a web API.

00:02:25.175 --> 00:02:30.726
Add an HTTP API connection, name it Sales API, and enter its Base URL:

00:02:30.726 --> 00:02:33.939
the address every request starts with.

00:02:34.390 --> 00:02:40.543
Authentication offers none, an API key, basic, a bearer token, OAuth 2.0, or

00:02:40.543 --> 00:02:45.496
OpenID Connect, and the fields below change with the choice.

00:02:45.496 --> 00:02:48.798
This API wants a key, so pick API key.

00:02:48.798 --> 00:02:54.127
The header name is filled in with a common choice, and you change

00:02:54.127 --> 00:02:56.828
it if the API expects another.

00:02:56.828 --> 00:02:58.630
Then type the key.

00:02:59.080 --> 00:03:00.548
Click Save and test.

00:03:00.548 --> 00:03:02.411
This time the check is red.

00:03:02.411 --> 00:03:06.194
The site is not allowed to reach a server on its own network,

00:03:06.194 --> 00:03:08.509
unless its administrator allows it.

00:03:08.509 --> 00:03:10.994
The message is for your administrator:

00:03:10.994 --> 00:03:13.140
nothing in this form can fix it.

00:03:17.331 --> 00:03:22.269
Once the administrator has allowed the address, open the connection again.

00:03:22.269 --> 00:03:24.799
The key's box doesn't show the key.

00:03:24.799 --> 00:03:29.120
It says the key is saved, and to leave the box empty to keep it.

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:34.181
Passwords, keys and secrets are stored encrypted, and are never shown again.

00:03:34.632 --> 00:03:38.963
Leave a secret box empty to keep the stored secret, or type a

00:03:38.963 --> 00:03:40.773
new one to replace it.

00:03:40.773 --> 00:03:42.842
Click Save and test again.

00:03:43.293 --> 00:03:47.353
Now the message is green, and says what the server answered.

00:03:51.543 --> 00:03:55.567
Each connection in the list shows its kind and technical name.

00:03:55.567 --> 00:03:58.584
Open Actions on a row to see what you can do:

00:03:58.584 --> 00:04:04.383
Test checks a saved connection again, without opening it, and the result appears at the top.

00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:09.154
Add data set starts a data set that reads from this connection.

00:04:09.606 --> 00:04:12.010
And Delete removes a connection.

00:04:12.010 --> 00:04:14.731
But not while data sets read from it:

00:04:14.731 --> 00:04:19.666
the site refuses, and tells you to remove or move those data sets first.

00:04:23.866 --> 00:04:30.051
An Elasticsearch connection takes the cluster's URL and one of four ways to authenticate.

00:04:30.051 --> 00:04:32.916
OpenSearch clusters work the same way.

00:04:33.367 --> 00:04:38.700
A SQL Server connection takes the server's name, the database, and how to sign in:

00:04:38.700 --> 00:04:43.549
with a SQL Server login, with the site's Windows account, with a Microsoft

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:47.307
Entra identity, or with the identity of the site's host.

00:04:47.829 --> 00:04:52.503
It can also encrypt the traffic, trust a certificate the site can't check,

00:04:52.503 --> 00:04:57.529
and read from a read-only replica, so reports don't slow the main database down.

00:04:57.988 --> 00:05:02.206
Give every connection a login that can only read what reports need:

00:05:02.206 --> 00:05:05.498
a data set reads with the rights of its connection.

00:05:07.498 --> 00:05:11.731
A connection is set up once per system, checked with Save and test,

00:05:11.731 --> 00:05:13.760
and keeps its secrets hidden.

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.311
Next, add data sets that read from it.

00:05:16.311 --> 00:05:17.818
Thanks for watching.

